WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our first guests tonight

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:03.869
are one of Hollywood's
funniest couples

00:00:03.869 --> 00:00:05.004
who you know from their films
like, "Thunder Force,"

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:07.039
"Superintelligence,"
"The Boss," and "Tammy."

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:08.274
They produced the documentary,

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:11.144
"Bob Ross: Happy Accidents,
Betrayal &amp; Greed,"

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:12.946
which begins streaming
on Netflix tomorrow.

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:13.979
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:15.381
-Bob liked the thrill

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:19.252
of watching a new student smile

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:22.221
with a fresh, new,
beautiful painting.

00:00:22.221 --> 00:00:24.390 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.727
Bob would just make them
so happy, it's -- it's unreal.

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:29.728 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:29.728 --> 00:00:31.797
It's like, "Man, did I do this?

00:00:31.797 --> 00:00:33.866
I couldn't have."

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:36.335
"Yes, you did."

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:38.538
-There's a lot going on
besides painting.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:42.041
And I think people are aware
of that, and they enjoy that.

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:43.242
-Please welcome to the show

00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:44.944
Melissa McCarthy
and Ben Falcone.

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:46.646
It's lovely to see you both.

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:49.081
-Hi, how are you?
-Hi! Good to see you.

00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:51.550
-So -- First of all, like
I said, it's great to see you.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:52.786
Melissa, you've been
on the show before.

00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:53.986
Ben, it is your first time.

00:00:53.986 --> 00:00:56.623
I am so happy to have you guys
here together.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:01:00.627
And together is where you spent
most of, I guess, the last year

00:01:00.627 --> 00:01:03.396
in Australia.
How did it treat you?

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.164
[ Both sigh ]
-Very nicely.

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:07.767
I have to say,
we kind of really --

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.936
We got in there
at an amazing time.

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.370
And at first,
when they were like, you know,

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.006
"Would you consider going
to Australia to work?"

00:01:14.006 --> 00:01:17.210
I was like, "No. Like, we --
We can't go down to the street.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.512
I can't move a family across
the world. That's insane."

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:21.714
-Yeah, we can't go to Australia
if we can't go to Trader Joe's.

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:25.384
-"This is nuts. We can't go to
Trader Joe's, we can't go to --"

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:29.121
And then our 14-year-old's like,
"We should leave tonight.

00:01:29.121 --> 00:01:31.624
Get me out of Zoom classes.
We should leave tonight."

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:35.161
We were like...
"Wait, let me call somebody."

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:38.364
And it was kind of great,
you know? It's a friendly --

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:42.401
It's a chatty, friendly --

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:43.903
-Very Midwestern kind of time.

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:45.571
-Yeah, we're both Midwestern.

00:01:45.571 --> 00:01:47.506
Like, you know, you go down
a grocery aisle,

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:49.876
and you're going to have
like three full conversations

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:52.445
just about random stuff.
-Yeah, about bananas.

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:54.646
"You like those bananas?"
-See, this is great.

00:01:54.646 --> 00:01:56.782
I was going to ask if you guys
can do the accent now.

00:01:56.782 --> 00:01:58.017
That was really good, Ben.

00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:00.352
Melissa, I heard
you can't do it.

00:02:00.352 --> 00:02:02.088
-[ Gasps ] I think --

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:03.356
I think I can do a few things.

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:05.290
But then any Australian's like,
"That's awful."

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.060
I like, "Noyr."

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:10.630
'Cause they put an O-Y-R
at the end of the "Noyr."

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.265
And then I-I --
You shave with "rayzor blaydes."

00:02:13.265 --> 00:02:14.634
-Oh, that's --

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:17.503
-Razor blades really fast is,

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:19.305
"Ah, I've got
some razor blades."

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.674
-"Razor blades." Max said,
"Don't move your mouth."

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:23.209
-This is very, very helpful.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:26.613
Hey, I want to ask this. When
you guys travel across the world

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:28.714
or whenever you travel together,
because, Ben,

00:02:28.714 --> 00:02:31.751
you famously played an air
marshal in "Bridesmaids" --

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:34.420
for my money, one of
the funniest movie scenes

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.988
that ever took place
on an airplane.

00:02:35.988 --> 00:02:37.323
When you guys travel together,

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:40.026
is it jarring for other people
on the flight?

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.960
[ Both laugh ]

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.564
-I think they're always
a little bit like, "Ha, ha!"

00:02:45.564 --> 00:02:48.401
Or they're like, "Oh, my God.

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:49.903
You guys got married after
that?"

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:51.336
And I was like,
"Well, we were married."

00:02:51.336 --> 00:02:52.638
They're like, "Right."

00:02:52.638 --> 00:02:55.541
-One time, a drunk guy did say,

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:57.677
"Hey, you going to
keep us safe?"

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:00.279
And I was like, "Yeah, you know,
I'll try to."

00:03:00.279 --> 00:03:02.448
And he goes, "Seriously.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:03.782
Are you are going to
keep us safe?"

00:03:03.782 --> 00:03:06.152
I was like --
What do you say to that?

00:03:06.152 --> 00:03:07.453
I was like, "It was a movie."

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:08.888
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:12.058
-You mentioned
you were both Midwesterners.

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:13.793
And -- And I was aware
that you guys met

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:16.262
working together
at the Groundlings in L.A.

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.064
But then, you actually
hadn't met then.

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:22.334
You had met back when you were
growing up in -- in Illinois.

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:25.871
-We had had an encounter.
I think I can call it that,

00:03:25.871 --> 00:03:27.240
which was --
I've never called it that.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.974
-Ooh. So mysterious.
-Ooh!

00:03:28.974 --> 00:03:30.109
We're breaking news here.

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:33.146
-It's getting very nighttime.

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:37.483
We -- I went to college
at SIU Carbondale,

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:40.719
Southern Illinois,
which is where Ben grew up.

00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:42.522
He's three years
younger than me.

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:45.858
So I was a freshman when he was
still in high school.

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:49.595
And when we became friends
in L.A.,

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:52.064
like a week into it,
he was like, "Oh, my God.

00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:53.332
I know who you were."

00:03:53.332 --> 00:03:54.934
And I was like, "No, you don't.

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.602
You would never recognize me.
No, you --"

00:03:56.602 --> 00:03:59.772
And he's like, "No, I did."
I-I think I know who you were."

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:02.508
And he goes,
"No, I was afraid of you."

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:05.310
And I was like, "Oh, my God.
That was me."

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.746
-Yeah. Once -- Once I mentioned

00:04:07.746 --> 00:04:09.815
that she struck terror
in certain people,

00:04:09.815 --> 00:04:10.983
she was like,
"Yep, that was me."

00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:13.252
'Cause she was very,
very goth, very --

00:04:13.252 --> 00:04:15.622
And all my, you know, friends
who were of that age

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:18.524
went, "Oh, my God. I wish my mom
would let me dress like her."

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:21.427
-And really, all I was doing
is I was so hot

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:24.196
because in Southern Illinois
in, like, August,

00:04:24.196 --> 00:04:28.668
to commit to a full-length cape
and, like, opaque tights,

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:32.371
you're digging in
in a way that nobody should.

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:34.940
-So, Ben, the first time
you laid eyes on your wife,

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.142
she was caped.

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:37.610
Is this what we're hearing?

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.445
-Yeah. It was probably
the dead of summer,

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.380
and she had a very heavy cape

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:45.884
and kind of a nice --
a nice shade of blue hair.

00:04:45.884 --> 00:04:47.052
-That's a -- you know?

00:04:47.052 --> 00:04:49.388
-And his eyes
just went to hearts.

00:04:49.388 --> 00:04:51.257
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.257 --> 00:04:53.559
-So, you guys started --

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:56.395
I think a lot of us had to find
ways to entertain ourselves

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:57.963
over the past 18 months.

00:04:57.963 --> 00:05:00.966
You started a movie club
with some of your friends,

00:05:00.966 --> 00:05:02.702
a thematic movie club.

00:05:02.702 --> 00:05:04.770
And you were, like, very much
at the beginning of it,

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.774
because you've really
charged through a lot of films.

00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:11.777
-It started with,
we were just like, "We're not --

00:05:11.777 --> 00:05:14.747
I'm just scrubbing fruit all day
with Clorox wipes,"

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:16.382
which, by the way,
don't do that.

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:18.650
But in the beginning, I think
we were all like, "I don't know.

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.053
How do you -- How --
How clean is clean?"

00:05:21.053 --> 00:05:24.056
And we were realizing, like, we
weren't having any connection

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:27.793
to anybody when we were all so,
I mean, as we all did.

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:28.961
And so we're like, "Let's Zoom,

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:30.329
and we'll have, like,
Friday night drinks

00:05:30.329 --> 00:05:31.797
with a couple people."

00:05:31.797 --> 00:05:33.833
And then, literally, they're
like, "Oh, well, we all know --

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:35.567
Let's add a couple people,
add a couple people."

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:37.704
And Ben said --

00:05:37.704 --> 00:05:39.171
He's like,
"My brain's going to mush.

00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:40.606
I need to do something.

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:43.608
I'm going to start and watch
every single Best Picture winner

00:05:43.608 --> 00:05:45.010
from the Academy Awards."

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:48.013
-Yeah, from --
We decided in 1960.

00:05:48.013 --> 00:05:49.982
And it's that sort of thing. You
know, when the pandemic started,

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:51.517
we were all going
to take piano lessons

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:53.252
and learn a bunch of languages.
And I'm gonna --

00:05:53.252 --> 00:05:55.588
"Oh, trig? You got it.
Trigonometry? I got it."

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:56.756
-I'm gonna get my master's!

00:05:56.756 --> 00:05:58.624
Although I never got past
freshman year

00:05:58.624 --> 00:06:00.393
in regular college,
but whatever.

00:06:00.393 --> 00:06:02.762
-But, so --
But it actually is because --

00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:05.264
Because it turned into this
really fun thing, we've --

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:06.865
I think we've watched, you know,

00:06:06.865 --> 00:06:08.934
definitely over 50
Best Picture winners.

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:11.837
-No, I think we're into --
We're in -- We're past 55.

00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:13.205
-Yeah, yeah. It started at --

00:06:13.205 --> 00:06:16.108
-Is there one that --
Is there one you can say,

00:06:16.108 --> 00:06:17.976
"Hey, you probably
haven't seen this one.

00:06:17.976 --> 00:06:22.047
It's better than you heard.
You should definitely watch it"?

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:24.517
-Ooh, there's --
There's a lot of great ones.

00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:28.620
-I think just remembering
how, like, rewatching

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.856
things that you've seen
so many times but you didn't --

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:32.925
And, like, we watch it
during the week on our own,

00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:35.894
and then we meet.
There's a -- There's a Q&amp;A.

00:06:35.894 --> 00:06:37.530
-Great.
-There are costumes,

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.031
and there are prizes.
-Trivia components.

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:41.801
-Very stupid prizes given.

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:44.670
But I think, like, watching
"Terms of Endearment" again --

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.871
-Yeah.

00:06:45.871 --> 00:06:47.873
-...watching
"Kramer vs. Kramer."

00:06:47.873 --> 00:06:49.642
-"In the Heat of the Night."
Sidney Poitier.

00:06:49.642 --> 00:06:51.010
-Oh, my God.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:53.679
-All three of those are --
Those are all Marvel movies.

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:55.747
People don't remember.
-Yes, exactly.

00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:58.817
And then to sit there
and actually having fun

00:06:58.817 --> 00:07:01.120
but also, people being like --
watching a movie,

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.389
talking about it with a bunch
of different filmmakers

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:07.326
and actors and producers
and writers, like, you don't --

00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:10.096
You know, you don't really
get to do that since college.

00:07:10.096 --> 00:07:12.197
And, like, it's just been --
It's been so fun.

00:07:12.197 --> 00:07:13.566
-So it's a lot of laughs.

00:07:13.566 --> 00:07:16.135
And you sort of by accident
learn a lot of stuff, too.

00:07:16.135 --> 00:07:20.440
-And then there's a few
that you're like, "Aah.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:22.141
Sorry, but --"
-We won't name names,

00:07:22.141 --> 00:07:23.642
but there are
some turds in there.

00:07:23.642 --> 00:07:24.676
-"Patton"?

00:07:24.676 --> 00:07:25.912
-There are some turd
Best Pictures.

00:07:25.912 --> 00:07:27.980
-Oh, she named the name.
-Sorry, but "Patton"?

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:29.281
-He said we weren't
gonna name names.

00:07:29.281 --> 00:07:31.850
-It starts -- He's got
toothpaste on his eyebrows.

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:33.519
-[ Laughing ] No, he doesn't.

00:07:33.519 --> 00:07:35.621
-Oh! My God.

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:38.256
All respect,
but you start out a movie

00:07:38.256 --> 00:07:40.993
with toothpaste on --
on somebody's close-up,

00:07:40.993 --> 00:07:42.360
and I'm like, "I'm out.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:44.062
-It was not too great.

00:07:44.062 --> 00:07:46.632
-Hey, so you guys have been
writing partners

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:47.966
for a long time.

00:07:47.966 --> 00:07:50.402
I don't know. Is this the
first -- Is the Bob Ross doc

00:07:50.402 --> 00:07:53.472
the first time you guys have
produced a documentary together?

00:07:53.472 --> 00:07:54.539
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:07:54.539 --> 00:07:57.643
-And so, what brought it about?

00:07:57.643 --> 00:07:59.911
-Well, you know, I'm super
interested in Bob Ross

00:07:59.911 --> 00:08:01.847
and have loved him
for a long time.

00:08:01.847 --> 00:08:04.984
And I actually wanted
to write a biopic about him,

00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:07.219
half because I'm interested

00:08:07.219 --> 00:08:09.688
and half because she's always
wanted me to have a perm.

00:08:09.688 --> 00:08:11.089
-[ Sighs ] I really do.

00:08:11.089 --> 00:08:14.893
-So I was -- And I did
your basic, you know, cursory,

00:08:14.893 --> 00:08:16.195
how -- What do you find --

00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:18.463
You know, we no longer
go to the microfiche.

00:08:18.463 --> 00:08:20.266
You know, you go just
and look on Google

00:08:20.266 --> 00:08:23.335
and like, "Alright,
Bob Ross news" or whatever.

00:08:23.335 --> 00:08:25.271
And there's nothing there.

00:08:25.271 --> 00:08:27.907
So it was really interesting
to find, in this day and age,

00:08:27.907 --> 00:08:30.509
you can find --
I'm sure if we google you,

00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:32.478
there's zillions
of pages of stuff.

00:08:32.478 --> 00:08:34.579
You'll be like, "I don't even
know what some of this is."

00:08:34.579 --> 00:08:36.082
But there was nothing.

00:08:36.082 --> 00:08:37.083
-Yeah, which was just so --

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:38.684
We were like, "Wait, how --

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:41.453
How can anybody
that -- that well-known,

00:08:41.453 --> 00:08:44.156
you know, for this many years,"

00:08:44.156 --> 00:08:47.292
there's only like three
small bites of information.

00:08:47.292 --> 00:08:50.395
So it just made us really
kind of start to wonder

00:08:50.395 --> 00:08:51.831
what is the story there?

00:08:51.831 --> 00:08:54.466
And then we met these, you know,
great documentary filmmakers.

00:08:54.466 --> 00:08:55.735
And they were like,

00:08:55.735 --> 00:08:57.636
you know, "Are you guys thinking
of doing anything?"

00:08:57.636 --> 00:09:00.506
And they -- They had just
done "Lorena," and --

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:03.442
And then, you know, we said,
"What -- What about Bob Ross?"

00:09:03.442 --> 00:09:05.911
He's kind of --
Like, we're currently down,

00:09:05.911 --> 00:09:09.214
like, this wormhole
of, like, what is the story?"

00:09:09.214 --> 00:09:11.417
And they just -- They loved it.
And they went --

00:09:11.417 --> 00:09:13.920
It's such a different process.
They went right into, kind of,

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.454
this, like,
investigative research.

00:09:16.454 --> 00:09:19.191
We're going. It's feet on
the ground, knocking on doors.

00:09:19.191 --> 00:09:20.826
It was like -- It was exciting.

00:09:20.826 --> 00:09:22.161
-And there's a certain
poetry to it,

00:09:22.161 --> 00:09:24.964
because the way
that our filmmakers,

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:27.599
Joshua and Steven, you know,
described it to us,

00:09:27.599 --> 00:09:29.935
they said, you know,
sort of something akin to,

00:09:29.935 --> 00:09:32.204
"You know, the movie
reveals itself to you."

00:09:32.204 --> 00:09:36.142
And I was like,
"Well, we should do that."

00:09:36.142 --> 00:09:38.644
-And I was like, "That's
what I was going to say."

00:09:38.644 --> 00:09:39.945
-It really does, though.

00:09:39.945 --> 00:09:41.847
And it's -- It is fascinating,
like, what you say,

00:09:41.847 --> 00:09:44.583
I think we all just assume
we know everything about him.

00:09:44.583 --> 00:09:47.620
And then you realize you don't
actually know a single detail

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:49.888
other than his hair
or that he paints,

00:09:49.888 --> 00:09:51.556
and it's really
a wonderful documentary

00:09:51.556 --> 00:09:54.693
because of how much we find out.
-Oh, thanks.

00:09:54.693 --> 00:09:56.395
-Oh, good.
Glad you like it, Seth.

00:09:56.395 --> 00:09:58.797
-You guys, it is so lovely
to see you, as always.

00:09:58.797 --> 00:09:59.998
I wish you the best,

00:09:59.998 --> 00:10:02.201
and hopefully next time
I can see you in person.

00:10:02.201 --> 00:10:03.536
-Absolutely.
-I hope the same thing.

00:10:03.536 --> 00:10:04.770
-Your hair looks fantastic.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:06.338
-Still great hair!

00:10:06.338 --> 00:10:08.374
[ Laughs ]

00:10:08.374 --> 00:10:10.743
-"Bob Ross: Happy Accidents,
Betrayal &amp; Greed"

00:10:10.743 --> 00:10:12.177
begins streaming
on Netflix tomorrow,

00:10:12.177 --> 00:10:14.580
and "Nine Perfect Strangers"
is streaming now on Hulu.

